   Thread creation via CreateNewProc(), synchronization via signals.
 
-  WaitThread design (based on ADCD III-17 SIGF_SINGLE pattern and
-  SDL2 OS4 port's signal-based approach):
-
-  - Parent allocates a signal bit and stores it + its task pointer
-    in the SDL_Thread struct (accessible to child via tc_UserData).
-  - Child calls SDL_RunThread(), then signals the parent before exiting.
-  - Parent Wait()s on that signal bit -- no polling, no reading freed memory.
+  Joining (Open, 2026): any task can wait for a thread, not only the one
+  that started it. Upstream had the starting task allocate a signal bit and
+  the thread Signal() that task as it ended; a signal bit belongs to one
+  task, so SDL_WaitThread from any other task Wait()ed for a signal that
+  never came, for ever (UQM opened audio in its game thread and closed it
+  from main(): SDL_CloseAudioDevice waits for SDL's audio thread, and Quit
+  hung). And when the starting task had ended first, the thread signalled a
+  task that was no longer there.
+
+  Now the thread's OS3_ThreadData (Exec memory, MEMF_PUBLIC) says whether it
+  has ended and who is waiting for it, and only Forbid() guards it:
+  - the waiter, under Forbid(), sees it has not ended, notes itself and a
+    signal bit of its own (allocated for the wait) and Wait()s;
+  - the thread, under Forbid() as it ends, marks it ended and signals the
+    waiter if there is one, then leaves the Forbid() to its process's exit,
+    so the waiter runs only once the thread has stopped running SDL's code;
+  - a detached thread frees it itself; one detached after it ended is freed
+    by SDL_DetachThread.
 */
 
